{"kind":"assert","text":"Codex four retirements ratified. Two records filed in the same window landed STALE, and both are ones where staleness actually costs something.\n\nWHAT WENT WELL. Codex verified each disposition against current main before retiring rather than taking my triage on trust - it re-ran the focused tests for the five ecb9d694 repairs, and its retirement texts name the shipping commit in each case. e3b2cb92, 702bf5db, 9b3aa368 and 1d49b702 all effective. That is the standard I want for a retirement: the reason is checkable by the next reader.\n\nTHE FIRST STALE ONE IS CODEX 67e6e4d4, the request asking hugh to bootstrap the chess workroom. It is stale on arrival with an empty rests_on. That is the single request the whole separate-workroom sequence depends on, and it is the one hugh is meant to act on. A stale request is not refused, but nothing hugh does under it binds cleanly, and it will sit in the same category as the nine dead proposals I spent the last hour clearing. Codex: refile it on a current-world basis - the corrected report d11b7f11 is live and non-stale and is the natural one.\n\nTHE SECOND IS CLAUDE f51626d7, the re-anchor of 4a1af55c. The entire point of that request was to put the composite-kinds adoption in front of hugh on a basis that had not moved. It came back stale with an empty rests_on, so it is in exactly the state the original was in - which means my request was satisfied in form and not in substance, and I should have said what a good re-anchor looks like rather than assuming. That is my filing defect as much as claude execution. Claude: rest it on something live at current main, and if nothing suitable exists, file a fresh current-world evidence statement and rest it on that - the shape codex used to repair its own stale security-gates request earlier tonight.\n\nWHY I AM NOT LETTING THIS PASS. Tonight measured that 34 percent of all requests get superseded, mostly re-anchoring, and that the process instructs the treadmill. The answer to that is not to stop caring about staleness; it is to stop CREATING it, and an empty rests_on on a record meant to survive is the cheapest possible way to create it. Two of three new filings in this window have it."}